      { "target": "Nenda moja kwa moja.", "base": "Go straight on." },
      { "target": "Unaenda wapi?", "base": "Where are you going?" },
      { "target": "Nauli ni bei gani?", "base": "How much is the fare?" },
      { "target": "Simama hapa, tafadhali.", "base": "Stop here, please." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "iko-wapi",
        "title": "Iko wapi?",
        "body": [
          "To ask where a place or thing is, put **iko wapi?** after it: **Benki iko wapi?** (where is the bank?). The answer uses **iko** too: **Iko pale** (it’s over there).",
          "Strictly, **iko** changes with the noun’s class (**Msikiti uko wapi?**), but **iko** is understood everywhere and is what you’ll hear on the street."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ni-going",
        "title": "Ni-na-: I am …ing",
        "body": [
          "Swahili verbs are built from pieces. **Ni-na-enda** is **ni** (I) + **na** (now, present) + **enda** (go): “I am going”. Swap the first piece for “you”: **u-na-enda** (you are going).",
          "Add **-ni** to a place to mean “at” or “to” it: **shule** → **shuleni** (to school), **kazi** → **kazini** (to work), **nyumba** → **nyumbani** (home)."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Place", "To / at the place", "English"],
          ["shule", "shuleni", "school"],
          ["kazi", "kazini", "work"],
          ["nyumba", "nyumbani", "home"],
          ["mji", "mjini", "town"]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Matatu, daladala, boda boda",
        "body": [
          "Shared minibuses are called **matatu** in Kenya and Uganda and **daladala** in Tanzania. Motorbike taxis are **boda boda** (from “border to border”) or **piki piki**. Agree on the **nauli** (fare) before you set off, and say **Simama hapa!** when you want to get off."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
